{
  "dom_id": "step-q121717",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=\u27e8\\sqrt{4t+1},3t-2\u27e9,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4t+1}},3\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4(2)+1}},3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}},3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{3},3\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
